# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 3.0754e+04 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 2.2830 79.814 1.0468e+06 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~80 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 8.4270 26.201 2.3519e+09 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 6 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 - drag_overtakes_ablation 8.4282 26.176 2.3574e+09 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
5 F flare 8.4434 26.058 6.6206e+09 stylized  Light rises x6.9 in 0.11 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 26.2 km (87% of m0); split gen 0 at 26.0 km (86% of m0). Dust groups supply 60% of the rise and 52% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
6 B first_breakup 8.4537 25.979 1.0982e+10 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.7 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
7 P peak 8.4567 25.959 2.3188e+10 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.7 km from the peak of energy deposition (25.2 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 13.3 km/s).
8 D rolloff 8.6067 24.824 8.4840e+09 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.15 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 83% of v0, 72% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
9 T tail 10.1577 17.365 2.2891e+07 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
